<main id="main">
  <section class="bg-immunotherapy-bg py-24 md:py-28 bg-[top_center] bg-cover text-center relative" aria-labelledby="page-title">
    <div class="container">
      <h1 id="page-title" class="text-custom-gray-300 font-medium">Immunotherapy</h1>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="im-intro" aria-labelledby="intro-title">
    <div class="container editorial-layout">
      <div class="editorial-copy">
        <h2 class="im-heading" id="intro-title">How can the immune system fight cancer?</h2>
        <p>Cancer cells can escape immune detection. Immunotherapy helps immune cells recognize cancer or strengthens their ability to attack it.</p>
        <a class="theme-btn orange-btn progress-cta" href="#types">Explore immunotherapy types <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
      </div>
      <section class="approaches-section" aria-labelledby="approaches-title">
        <h3 id="approaches-title">How cancer treatments work</h3>
        <div class="approach-grid">
          <article>
            <h4>Chemotherapy and radiation</h4>
            <p>Chemotherapy kills or slows the growth of fast-growing cells, while radiation damages cells in the area being treated. Both can affect healthy cells and cause side effects.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h4>Immunotherapy and vaccines</h4>
            <p>Immunotherapy helps the immune system act against cancer. Treatment vaccines are one approach. They help the immune system recognize targets on cancer cells.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="im-section types-section" aria-labelledby="types-title" id="types">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 class="im-heading" id="types-title">Types of Immunotherapy</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">Select a treatment to see how it works.</p>
      <div class="type-layout">
        <nav class="type-nav" aria-label="Immunotherapy types"><a id="tab-checkpoint" href="#checkpoint">Immune Checkpoint Inhibitors<span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a id="tab-t-cells" href="#t-cells">T-Cell Transfer Therapy<span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a id="tab-antibodies" href="#antibodies">Monoclonal Antibodies<span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a id="tab-vaccines" href="#vaccines">Treatment Vaccines<span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a id="tab-modulators" href="#modulators">Immune System Modulators<span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></nav>
        <div class="type-panels"><article class="type-panel" id="checkpoint"><div class="type-copy"><h3>Immune Checkpoint Inhibitors</h3><p>Immune checkpoints keep immune responses in check. Some cancers use these signals to evade immune attack. Checkpoint inhibitors block these signals so immune cells can respond more strongly.</p><p>Examples include pembrolizumab (Keytruda) and nivolumab (Opdivo).</p></div><figure class="treatment-figure"><img src="/assets/images/redesign/immunotherapy/immtype1.png" alt="Diagram comparing immune checkpoint signaling with and without PD-1 or PD-L1 inhibition" loading="lazy"><figcaption><a href="/assets/images/redesign/immunotherapy/immtype1.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View full-size diagram <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></figcaption></figure></article><article class="type-panel" id="t-cells"><div class="type-copy"><h3>T-Cell Transfer Therapy</h3><p>This approach uses a patient’s own immune cells to fight cancer. In CAR T-cell therapy, a form of T-cell transfer, the patient’s T cells are modified to recognize a target on cancer cells and then returned to the patient.</p></div><figure class="treatment-figure"><img src="/assets/images/redesign/immunotherapy/immtype2.png" alt="Diagram showing the stages of CAR T-cell therapy" loading="lazy"><figcaption><a href="/assets/images/redesign/immunotherapy/immtype2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View full-size diagram <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></figcaption></figure></article><article class="type-panel" id="antibodies"><div class="type-copy"><h3>Monoclonal Antibodies</h3><p>These lab-made proteins bind to specific targets. Some attach to cancer cells and mark them for destruction by the immune system.</p></div><figure class="treatment-figure"><img src="/assets/images/redesign/immunotherapy/immtype3.png" alt="Diagram of monoclonal antibodies attaching to a cell surface" loading="lazy"><figcaption><a href="/assets/images/redesign/immunotherapy/immtype3.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View full-size diagram <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></figcaption></figure></article><article class="type-panel" id="vaccines"><div class="type-copy"><h3>Treatment Vaccines</h3><p>Cancer treatment vaccines aim to help the immune system recognize and respond to cancer that is already present. Preventive vaccines work earlier by protecting against certain infections that can lead to cancer.</p></div><figure><img src="/assets/images/redesign/immunotherapy/immtype5.jpg" alt="Rows of vaccine vials" loading="lazy"></figure></article><article class="type-panel" id="modulators"><div class="type-copy"><h3>Immune System Modulators</h3><p>Immune system modulators help increase immune activity against cancer. Some act on specific parts of the immune system, while others have a broader effect.</p></div><figure><img src="/assets/images/redesign/immunotherapy/immtype4.jpg" alt="A gloved hand holding a vial labeled BCG" loading="lazy"></figure></article></div>
      </div>
      <p class="source-line"><a href="https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/types/immunotherapy">Read more at the National Cancer Institute <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

